component SigilCompilationProfile {
  goal {
    Own built-in and project compiler profiles, evaluator selection, stage
    dependency closure, budgets, and reproducible effective-profile identity.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationProfile {
      resolve(name, toolsCompile, requestedStage?) returns ordered stages,
      evaluators, skills, capabilities, execution budgets, resource limits, and an
      effective-profile fingerprint or rejects an unknown profile or stage with
      COMPILER_INVALID_INVOCATION and an unavailable required evaluator with a stable
      profile failure

      standard and critical-system are built in; project profiles may configure or
      omit stages subject to dependency rules

      tools.compile.adapter and each tools.compile.evaluators identity select
      provider codex, claude, opencode, or pi and may include one optional model
      string
    }

    AgentCapabilityContract {
      workspaceAccess, network, approvalEscalation, ephemeral execution, and every
      forbidden effect are preventive constraints that each adapter enforces without
      broadening

      allowedCommands is an exact settlement allowlist. Each emitted operation is
      classified at the earliest structured provider event containing its complete
      native identity and arguments, and only an allowed operation may enter a
      successful result

      when a provider exposes complete operation data only after execution starts or
      completes, rejecting the operation terminates the provider and discards the
      complete evaluation but does not claim that the read-only operation was
      prevented

      allowedCommands and forbiddenCommands contain only exact identifiers from a
      compiler-owned finite canonical command-family vocabulary

      commandFamilies contains the immutable resolved CanonicalCommandFamily
      descriptor for every allowed or forbidden identifier and no others
    }

    CanonicalCommandFamily {
      resolveCanonicalCommandFamily(identifier) returns one immutable descriptor
      containing operation kind, operand schema, exact option allowlist, path policy,
      and permitted effects, or rejects an unknown identifier

      inspection identifiers are workspace.read, workspace.glob, workspace.grep,
      workspace.list, sigil.version, sigil.parse, sigil.check, sigil.fmt-check,
      sigil.glossary, sigil.graph, sigil.context, sigil.render, git.status, git.diff,
      git.show, git.log, git.grep, and git.ls-files

      denial identifiers are sigil.init, sigil.fmt-write, sigil.compile,
      sigil.skill-install, network.client, filesystem.mutate, code.generate, and
      implementation.execute; no other command-family identifier is valid

      every inspection descriptor permits only workspace-root-confined reads and
      stdout or stderr emission, denies network and mutation, and defines all native
      operands and options that may map to it; denial descriptors permit no execution
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationProfile {
  logic {
    CompilationProfile {
      Merge built-in defaults with the selected tools.compile project profile.

      Resolve execution budgets from tools.compile.budgets and request/session limits
      from tools.compile.limits. Omitted fields use the compiler's
      backward-compatible defaults.

      Treat the singular adapter as the backward-compatible default evaluator and
      select configured evaluator identities through evaluatorIds.

      Validate only selected evaluator identities. Resolve requestedStage to its
      complete transitive dependency closure without enabling unrelated stages.

      Fingerprint effective stages, skills, adapters, models, capabilities, execution
      budgets, resource limits, disabled stages, and relevant versions.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ProfileConfiguration {
      ConfigFile tools.compile contains no credentials or secret values.

      tools.compile.budgets configures execution values and limits configures request
      sizes and proposal-session retention. Every configured value is a positive safe
      integer.

      Evaluator identities are unique; evaluatorIds contains no duplicates and
      references configured evaluators.

      Each selected evaluator declares provider codex, claude, opencode, or pi. Its
      optional model is a non-empty provider-native identifier and contains no
      credential or secret value.

      An omitted model delegates selection to the provider CLI. The compiler neither
      discovers provider catalogs nor substitutes another model when selection fails.

      Unselected evaluator configuration does not affect workspace loading or another
      profile.
    }

    StageConfiguration {
      Profiles configure stages subject to declared dependencies. Disabled stages are
      not required for green and remain visible in the report and fingerprint.

      Stage-scoped compilation cannot bypass dependencies or select a disabled stage.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      standard requires deterministic foundation and configured design evaluation
      through one evaluator.

      critical-system adds standards and risk evaluation, at least two distinct
      available evaluator identities, and material-disagreement detection.

      Invalid critical-system evaluator configuration fails only that selected run.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ConfigurableCompilationResources {
      Decision: Make execution budgets, evaluation request limits, and
      proposal-session retention explicit tools.compile configuration with
      backward-compatible omitted-field defaults.

      Scope: Governs compiler profile resolution, adapter request validation, and
      compilation-session expiry; excludes provider credentials and semantic
      evaluation policy.

      Trade-offs: Workspaces can size compilation for their available resources,
      while effective profiles retain the resolved values for auditability and
      reproducibility.

      Design issues addressed: Hidden source-level constants made resource policy
      vary without a visible workspace contract and prevented operators from
      adjusting safe limits for larger repositories.

      Consequences: Configuration changes alter the effective-profile fingerprint and
      can change acceptance of large requests, evaluator execution, and session
      retention.
    }

    SharedAgentCapabilityContract {
      Decision: Own the provider-neutral agent capability vocabulary in
      SigilCompilationProfile and require skills and adapters to consume that one
      resolved contract.

      Scope: Governs profile capability resolution and provider enforcement; excludes
      provider-specific CLI flags and event formats.

      Trade-offs: Capability vocabulary changes require profile fingerprint changes
      and coordinated consumer validation.

      Design issues addressed: Provider adapters must not define the policy they are
      responsible for enforcing.

      Consequences: Profiles remain the single capability-policy owner while each
      adapter independently proves it can enforce the resolved values.
    }

    ProviderModelSelection {
      Decision: Configure one optional opaque provider-native model on each adapter
      or evaluator identity and include it in effective-profile identity.

      Scope: Governs tools.compile provider and model selection; excludes
      credentials, provider model discovery, and fallback policy inside external
      CLIs.

      Trade-offs: Configuration remains stable across provider catalog changes, but a
      misspelled or unavailable model is detected only when its evaluator is
      selected.

      Design issues addressed: Implicit user-level model defaults make otherwise
      identical compiler runs select different evaluators without a profile change.

      Discarded alternatives: A compiler-owned model catalog and automatic fallback
      were rejected as volatile and non-reproducible.

      Consequences: The selected provider and configured model value affect the
      profile fingerprint, and provider rejection fails the selected evaluation. An
      omitted model reproduces configuration but not the provider's changing default.
    }

    ProfileScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Compute status against an exact named profile and environment
      fingerprint whose stages are configurable.

      Scope: Governs built-in and project profiles.

      Trade-offs: Green is never an unconditional property of a Sigil file.

      Consequences: Evaluation depth remains configurable and auditable.
    }

    StageScopedCompilation {
      Decision: Invoke one named stage with its complete dependency closure and no
      unrelated stages.

      Scope: Governs focused compiler runs and effective-profile identity.

      Trade-offs: Stage identifiers are reserved compile operands.

      Consequences: Readiness evaluation avoids unrelated stage cost and meaning.
    }

    CriticalEvaluatorRedundancy {
      Decision: Require at least two configured evaluator identities for every
      critical-system agentic stage and detect material disagreement.

      Scope: Governs critical-system profile validation and evaluator selection.

      Trade-offs: Critical runs cost more and may remain non-green on disagreement.

      Discarded alternatives: One evaluator or an extra stage was rejected as false
      redundancy.

      Consequences: Critical status records independent completion and disagreement.
    }
  }

  cases {
    StageConfiguration {
      A disabled optional stage can still permit green and is recorded.

      Requesting semantic-readiness executes deterministic-foundation first and no
      unrelated later stage.
    }

    ProviderModelSelection {
      A standard adapter configured as provider opencode with model
      anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5 resolves that exact evaluator identity.

      A Pi evaluator configured with model openai/gpt-5 preserves the complete model
      string in its effective-profile identity.

      An empty or non-string model, or an unsupported selected provider, rejects
      configuration.
    }

    BuiltInProfiles {
      Missing critical evaluator configuration does not prevent standard compilation.

      Selecting critical-system with fewer than two evaluators rejects with
      COMPILER_PROFILE_EVALUATORS_REQUIRED before agentic evaluation.
    }
  }
}
